<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>

<section xml:id="sec_sensors_actuators_pipeline" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Sensors, Actuators, and the Closed-Loop Robotics Pipeline</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>Robots operate by continuously gathering information, deciding on actions, executing commands, and verifying outcomes. This closed-loop pipeline enables adaptation when the environment changes. The cycle can be summarized as perception, planning, action, and verification.</p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_pipeline_stages">
    <title>Pipeline Stages</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Perception:</em> Collect and interpret sensor data.</p></li>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Planning:</em> Choose a goal-directed action strategy.</p></li>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Action:</em> Command actuators to execute motion.</p></li>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Verification:</em> Use feedback to check whether expected motion occurred.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_sensors_vs_actuators">
    <title>Sensors and Actuators</title>
    <p>Sensors are robot inputs. They provide state and environment information such as distance, orientation, location, and object identity. Common examples include cameras, LiDAR, encoders, GPS, IMUs, and distance sensors.</p>
    <p>Actuators are robot outputs. They convert electrical commands into physical motion and force. Common examples include DC motors, servos, pneumatic cylinders, grippers, and robotic arms.</p>
    <p>In VEX systems, distance and optical sensors support perception, while smart motors drive wheels, arms, and intake mechanisms.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_pipeline_examples">
    <title>Real-World and VEX Examples</title>
    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec_self_driving_pipeline">
      <title>Self-Driving Car</title>
      <p>Perception fuses camera, radar, LiDAR, and GPS data; planning computes route and maneuvers; action commands steering, throttle, and braking; verification checks actual speed, lane position, and steering response for correction.</p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec_warehouse_pipeline">
      <title>Warehouse Robot</title>
      <p>Perception uses vision, markers, and ranging sensors; planning optimizes path and avoids traffic; action drives and lifts shelves; verification uses encoder feedback and replanning when slip or blockage occurs.</p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec_vex_case_study">
      <title>VEX Case Study: Collecting a Game Object</title>
      <p>The robot senses object color and range, plans a path and approach distance, actuates drivetrain and intake, and verifies travel with encoders. A feedback controller adjusts motor power to correct drift or slip in real time.</p>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_pipeline_checks">
    <title>Concept Checks</title>
    <ol>
      <li><p>A correct closed-loop sequence is Sense -> Plan -> Act -> Verify.</p></li>
      <li><p>Encoder is a sensor; smart motor is an actuator.</p></li>
      <li><p>Encoders close the loop by reporting actual wheel rotation so the controller can correct error.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>
</section>
